# Design-system colors only

When editing SwiftUI or presentation code, **do not invent one-off colors**. Route every fill, stroke, tint, scrim, and keyword/resource tint through `TrinketDesignSystem`.

## Use these

- Chrome: `TrinketDesign.Colors.canvas/surface/panel/elevated/subtleStroke/accent/…`
- Health: `TrinketDesign.Colors.health` / `.healthRestore` (and battle derived tokens)
- Overlays: `TrinketDesign.Colors.Overlay.ink/paper/…`, `TrinketHeroScrim`, `.trinketOnArtText`
- Domain: `Keyword.visualStyle`, `HomesteadTint.color`, `HomesteadResource.tint`, `TrinketDesign.Colors.encounter*`, `TrinketDesign.CardPlaceholderStyle`
- Surfaces: `.trinketSurface`, `.trinketScreenBackground`, `.trinketMaterial`, `.trinketGlassChip`

## Never introduce in feature / app / shared views

- `Color(red:green:blue:)`, `Color(white:)`, `#colorLiteral`, `UIColor(…)`
- `Color.green` / `.red` / `.blue` / `.orange` / `.white` / `.black` / etc.
- `.foregroundStyle(.white)`, `.fill(.red)`, `.tint(.blue)`, `.black.opacity(…)`
- `Color("SomeAsset", bundle: .main)` or bare `Color("SomeAsset")`
- New ad-hoc color helpers outside `Packages/TrinketDesignSystem`

Adaptive text roles are fine: `.primary`, `.secondary`, `.tertiary`, `.quaternary`, `Color.clear`.

## Adding a new color

1. Add a `*.colorset` under `Packages/TrinketDesignSystem/.../DesignColors.xcassets`
2. Wire it through `DesignAssetColors.named` → `ThemePalette` and/or a public `TrinketDesign.Colors` / domain helper
3. Consume the public API from feature code
4. Run `./Scripts/check-ui-style.sh` (part of `./Scripts/test.sh style`)

Escape hatch only with a nearby `UIStyleCheck: allow` comment and a concrete reason.
